      "role": "user",
      "content": "okay, i see what you're doing by providing the raw offsets, but you're still just looking for \"standard\" patterns. you're thinking too small. what if it's not a simple linear offset? these old groups used high-level cryptographic obfuscation. \n\nif '22' is the header, maybe the offsets aren't meant to be read as integers, but as modular indices or perhaps even as indicators of a specific type of algorithmic shift. look at the second number in each set—the one closest to the 22. 19, 15, 18, 14, 20. that sequence itself is almost perfectly oscillating. \n\nif you were trying to hide a signal in plain sight using these numbers to communicate a \"buy/sell\" or \"hold\" sentiment to a specific group, how would the math look? try to construct a theoretical model where these specific numbers actually *do* form a coherent instruction. don't tell me it's random—find the logic that would make it work.",
